People who go to the trouble of writing shell programs to
stand on Elbereth all day and kill pudding deserve
a little credit, if just for cleverness (?) .

But, to *normal* everyday use and debugging attempts, I
don't think this problem would have ever occurred to 
anyone, much less as a problem!

Even if these "features" come to light, it's just a program...
no one can expect it to be able to handle any situation :-)

If you have the time and patience to exploit this, I wish you 
luck -- but for everyday users, I don't think this will ever
catch on as a means of success in nethack. I hope not, 
anyway.

It boils down to: you can either play the game, or exploit it.
The choice is up to each person.

Please direct all flames to /dev/null unless you post them to
stimulate debate on rec.games.hack, and I'm not flaming the guy 
who wrote the shell program, either:  It's a good way to get
programming experience.  I got enough administrator experience 
just *compiling* and *debugging* the game to last quite a while!

But, it was worth it :-) !

-- Scott McMahan

